The new node is created with `x` as its data and the current head as its `next` node. Returning it changes the starting point of the list.

A quick dry run with `10 -> 20` and `x = 4` gives `4 -> 10 -> 20`. If `head` is `null`, the result is a single node whose `next` is still `null`.

```javascript
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
    constructor(x, next){
        this.data = x;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

/**
 * @param {ListNode} head
 * @param {number} x
 * @return {ListNode}
 */

class Solution {
    insertAtHead(head, x) {
        const newNode = new ListNode(x, head);
        return newNode;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, excluding the new list node.

A front insertion only needs two links. The new node stores `X`, points to the current head, and becomes the returned head.

For an empty list, `head` is `nil`, so the new node simply points to `nil`. The code does not walk through any existing nodes.

```go
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
type ListNode struct {
    Data int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func InsertAtHead(head *ListNode, X int) *ListNode {
    newNode := &ListNode{
        Data: X,
        Next: head,
    }
    return newNode
}
```

The running time is `O(1)`, and the extra space is `O(1)` for the new node. Existing nodes are not moved.

The first node is changed by making a new node point to the current `head`. Then the new node is returned as the list's beginning.

This also handles a one-node list and an empty list without a separate branch. The original nodes are not changed, only the new first link is added.

```java
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
    public int data;
    public ListNode next;
    ListNode() { data = 0; next = null; }
    ListNode(int x) { data = x; next = null; }
    ListNode(int x, ListNode next) { data = x; this.next = next; }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode insertAtHead(ListNode head, int X) {
        ListNode newNode = new ListNode(X);
        newNode.next = head;
        return newNode;
    }
}
```

The operation takes `O(1)` time and uses `O(1)` extra space besides the node created for `X`.

A new node becomes the first node, so its `next` pointer should store the old head. After that, returning the new node gives the updated list.

For an empty list, the old head is `nullptr`, and the same logic still works. For example, with `1 -> 2` and `X = 5`, the links become `5 -> 1 -> 2`.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
  public:
    int data;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode() : data(0), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x) : data(x), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : data(x), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
    public:
        ListNode* insertAtHead(ListNode* &head, int X) {
            ListNode* newNode = new ListNode(X, head);
            head = newNode;
            return head;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the new node itself.

